const passages = /* #__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[{\"passageNumber\":1,\"title\":\"Community Leisure Center and Household Waste Recycling Directives\",\"subtitle\":\"Municipal public service information regarding facility access and curbside sorting protocols\",\"wordCount\":410,\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nThe Oakwood District Leisure Pavilion is open to residents seven days a week, from 06:30 to 22:00 on weekdays and 08:00 to 20:00 on weekends. To access subsidized municipal rates, patrons must present a valid resident utility statement or council tax notice dated within the previous three months upon initial biometric key-fob issuance. Children under twelve years of age must be accompanied in the aquatic facility by an adult aged eighteen or older at all times. Lockers operate via rechargeable digital cards; padlocks and loose currency tokens are strictly incompatible with the electronic locking mechanisms.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nUnder the district's revised environmental regulations, curbside household waste collection is organized into a four-stream sorting framework. Blue lidded bins accept uncontaminated paper goods, clean corrugated cardboard, and flattened aluminum food tins. Cardboard boxes must be folded flat and must not exceed one meter in length to prevent hopper blockages in compaction lorries. Wax-coated beverage cartons and grease-soaked pizza boxes are explicitly prohibited from blue bins and must be diverted to grey refuse bins.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nGreen bins are designated exclusively for raw garden trimmings, grass clippings, and small pruned tree branches under ten centimeters in diameter. Soil, treated building timber, and pet waste are hazardous contaminants that will result in bin collection refusal and a corrective warning sticker. Hazardous materials—including lithium batteries, domestic chemical solvents, used motor fluids, and fluorescent light tubes—must be deposited personally at the Central Civic Amenity Depot on Millfield Road on designated disposal Saturdays.\",\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? In boxes 1–7 on your answer sheet, write:\\nTRUE if the statement agrees with the information\\nFALSE if the statement contradicts the information\\nNOT GIVEN if there is no information on this\",\"questionType\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"g2-q1\",\"questionNumber\":1,\"prompt\":\"The Leisure Pavilion opens at the same time on weekdays and weekends.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states it opens at 06:30 on weekdays and 08:00 on weekends.\",\"evidence\":\"from 06:30 to 22:00 on weekdays and 08:00 to 20:00 on weekends.\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q2\",\"questionNumber\":2,\"prompt\":\"Utility bills older than three months cannot be used to prove residency for discount rates.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A specifies patrons must show a statement 'dated within the previous three months upon initial biometric key-fob issuance.'\",\"evidence\":\"patrons must present a valid resident utility statement or council tax notice dated within the previous three months\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q3\",\"questionNumber\":3,\"prompt\":\"Patrons can use standard metal padlocks on pavilion lockers.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A explicitly says 'padlocks and loose currency tokens are strictly incompatible with the electronic locking mechanisms.'\",\"evidence\":\"Lockers operate via rechargeable digital cards; padlocks and loose currency tokens are strictly incompatible with the electronic locking mechanisms.\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q4\",\"questionNumber\":4,\"prompt\":\"Cardboard boxes placed in blue bins must be under one meter in length.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains boxes 'must be folded flat and must not exceed one meter in length'.\",\"evidence\":\"Cardboard boxes must be folded flat and must not exceed one meter in length to prevent hopper blockages in compaction lorries.\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q5\",\"questionNumber\":5,\"prompt\":\"Greasy pizza takeaway boxes should be placed in the blue recycling bin.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B notes 'grease-soaked pizza boxes are explicitly prohibited from blue bins and must be diverted to grey refuse bins.'\",\"evidence\":\"Wax-coated beverage cartons and grease-soaked pizza boxes are explicitly prohibited from blue bins and must be diverted to grey refuse bins.\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q6\",\"questionNumber\":6,\"prompt\":\"Pet waste is accepted in green garden bins if sealed in biodegradable bags.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C states that 'Soil, treated building timber, and pet waste are hazardous contaminants that will result in bin collection refusal'.\",\"evidence\":\"Soil, treated building timber, and pet waste are hazardous contaminants that will result in bin collection refusal\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q7\",\"questionNumber\":7,\"prompt\":\"The Central Civic Amenity Depot charges a fee for disposing of used motor oil.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NOT GIVEN\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C states that motor fluids must be deposited at the Central Civic Amenity Depot on Saturdays, but does not mention any fee.\",\"evidence\":\"Hazardous materials—including lithium batteries, domestic chemical solvents, used motor fluids, and fluorescent light tubes—must be deposited personally at the Central Civic Amenity Depot\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":2,\"title\":\"Public Library Digital Lending and Coworking Facility Policies\",\"subtitle\":\"Regulations for member workstations, borrowing allowances, and quiet study bookings\",\"wordCount\":420,\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nRegistered cardholders of the Metropolitan Public Library system may borrow up to fifteen physical volumes and ten digital e-books or audiobooks simultaneously. Physical book loans are issued for twenty-one days, while multimedia DVD loans carry a seven-day borrowing limit. Electronic titles automatically expire from client reading devices at 23:59 on their scheduled due date, preventing the accrual of overdue financial penalties. Physical books renewed online or via the mobile application will be extended for an additional three weeks, provided no active reservation hold has been requested by another member.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nThe third-floor Innovation Lab houses twelve collaborative multimedia suites equipped with high-performance audio editing software, 3D printing equipment, and professional podcasting microphones. Access to these specialty studios is complimentary for library members; however, reservations must be booked online at least forty-eight hours in advance through the library patron portal. Members who fail to check in within fifteen minutes of their reserved start time forfeit their booking slot, which is then made available to walk-in patrons.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nThe silent reading concourse on the fourth floor enforces absolute noise restrictions. Mobile phone telephone conversations are strictly forbidden, and personal headphones must be adjusted to prevent sound leakage. Beverages in spill-proof thermos flasks or securely capped water bottles are permitted; open takeaway coffee cups and hot food items are forbidden and will be confiscated by floor wardens. Printing and copying stations operate on a prepaid account basis: standard monochrome prints cost five pence per page, while color reproductions incur a twenty pence per sheet tariff.\",\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 8–14 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"summary_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"g2-q8\",\"questionNumber\":8,\"prompt\":\"Members can borrow a maximum of ____ digital books or audiobooks at any one time.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"ten\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states members can borrow 'ten digital e-books or audiobooks simultaneously.'\",\"evidence\":\"borrow up to fifteen physical volumes and ten digital e-books or audiobooks simultaneously.\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q9\",\"questionNumber\":9,\"prompt\":\"The loan period for multimedia DVD items is ____ days.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"seven\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A says 'multimedia DVD loans carry a seven-day borrowing limit.'\",\"evidence\":\"while multimedia DVD loans carry a seven-day borrowing limit.\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q10\",\"questionNumber\":10,\"prompt\":\"Renewals are not permitted if another cardholder has placed a ____ on the book.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"reservation hold\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states renewal is extended 'provided no active reservation hold has been requested by another member.'\",\"evidence\":\"provided no active reservation hold has been requested by another member.\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q11\",\"questionNumber\":11,\"prompt\":\"Specialized multimedia suites must be booked at least ____ in advance.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"forty-eight hours\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains reservations must be made 'at least forty-eight hours in advance'.\",\"evidence\":\"reservations must be booked online at least forty-eight hours in advance through the library patron portal.\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q12\",\"questionNumber\":12,\"prompt\":\"Booked studio slots are relinquished if members arrive more than ____ late.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"fifteen minutes\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B notes 'Members who fail to check in within fifteen minutes of their reserved start time forfeit their booking slot'.\",\"evidence\":\"Members who fail to check in within fifteen minutes of their reserved start time forfeit their booking slot\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q13\",\"questionNumber\":13,\"prompt\":\"In the silent study concourse, drinks must be carried in spill-proof flasks or ____ bottles.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"securely capped\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes beverages are permitted in 'securely capped water bottles'.\",\"evidence\":\"Beverages in spill-proof thermos flasks or securely capped water bottles are permitted\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q14\",\"questionNumber\":14,\"prompt\":\"Standard black-and-white printing costs ____ per page.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"five pence\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C states 'standard monochrome prints cost five pence per page'.\",\"evidence\":\"standard monochrome prints cost five pence per page, while color reproductions incur a twenty pence per sheet tariff.\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":3,\"title\":\"The Evolution of the Modern Postal and Courier Service\",\"subtitle\":\"From courier horse relays to automated algorithmic sorting centers and electric delivery fleets\",\"wordCount\":780,\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nThe dissemination of written dispatches is as old as organized statehood itself. In the ancient Persian Empire, the royal courier network known as the Chapar Khaneh operated relay stations spaced one day's ride apart along the 2,500-kilometer Royal Road. Fresh horses and rested couriers were kept perpetually stationed; messages traversed the entire breadth of the realm in seven days—a speed described by the Greek historian Herodotus as unhampered by snow, rain, heat, or darkness. Similar imperial courier networks, such as the Roman Cursus Publicus and the Incan Chasqui runners who traversed Andean mountain suspension bridges on foot, were designed strictly to convey administrative military decrees rather than personal correspondence for ordinary citizens.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nThe democratization of postal communication emerged in the mid-nineteenth century, sparked by British social reformer Rowland Hill. Prior to Hill's reforms, postal tariffs were extortionate and convoluted: postage was calculated based on the number of paper sheets and the mileage traveled, and payment was collected from the recipient upon delivery rather than the sender. This led to widespread fraud, as recipients routinely inspected the exterior of letters to decipher prearranged code marks before refusing delivery to avoid paying the carrier fee. In 1840, Hill introduced the Uniform Penny Post, introducing a uniform flat rate of one penny for any domestic letter weighing under half an ounce, prepaid by the sender using the world's first adhesive postage stamp, the famous Penny Black.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nThe introduction of prepaid uniform pricing catalyzed an astronomical surge in mail volume. Within two decades of the Penny Black's launch, British mail volumes leaped from 76 million to over 600 million items annually. The postal service rapidly evolved into an indispensable societal artery, democratizing familial correspondence, facilitating commercial catalog trading, and enabling the publication of low-cost periodicals. Railway transport networks transformed delivery speeds; specialized Traveling Post Office railway carriages were introduced, allowing postal clerks to manually sort letters inside vibrating train carriages while speeding between metropolitan rail termini.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nIn the contemporary era, the nature of postal logistics has undergone a complete structural inversion. As electronic mail, instant messaging, and digital invoicing rendered traditional physical letters virtually obsolete, the explosion of e-commerce catalyzed an exponential boom in parcel logistics. Modern fulfillment centers no longer resemble the manual sorting halls of the nineteenth century; today, automated hubs feature miles of optical barcode scanners, robotic sortation arms, and automated guided vehicles (AGVs) that process up to fifty thousand parcels an hour. Furthermore, environmental mandates are driving courier networks to replace diesel delivery vans with electric cargo bicycles and battery-electric fleets, ensuring that the ancient human urge to transmit goods evolves toward carbon-neutral resilience.\",\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 15–20 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"sentence_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"g2-q15\",\"questionNumber\":15,\"prompt\":\"The Persian royal courier system along the Royal Road was called the ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"Chapar Khaneh\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A identifies the network as 'the Chapar Khaneh'.\",\"evidence\":\"In the ancient Persian Empire, the royal courier network known as the Chapar Khaneh operated relay stations\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q16\",\"questionNumber\":16,\"prompt\":\"In the Incan empire, administrative messages were carried across mountain suspension bridges by ____ runners.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"Chasqui\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A mentions 'Incan Chasqui runners who traversed Andean mountain suspension bridges on foot'.\",\"evidence\":\"and the Incan Chasqui runners who traversed Andean mountain suspension bridges on foot\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q17\",\"questionNumber\":17,\"prompt\":\"Prior to Rowland Hill's postal reforms, postal charges were collected from the ____ upon arrival.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"recipient\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B states 'payment was collected from the recipient upon delivery rather than the sender.'\",\"evidence\":\"and payment was collected from the recipient upon delivery rather than the sender.\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q18\",\"questionNumber\":18,\"prompt\":\"The first adhesive postage stamp introduced in 1840 was called the ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"Penny Black\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B identifies 'the world's first adhesive postage stamp, the famous Penny Black.'\",\"evidence\":\"prepaid by the sender using the world's first adhesive postage stamp, the famous Penny Black.\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q19\",\"questionNumber\":19,\"prompt\":\"Clerks sorted letters on moving trains inside carriages known as the ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"Traveling Post Office\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C describes 'specialized Traveling Post Office railway carriages'.\",\"evidence\":\"specialized Traveling Post Office railway carriages were introduced, allowing postal clerks to manually sort letters inside vibrating train carriages\"},{\"id\":\"g2-q20\",\"questionNumber\":20,\"prompt\":\"Modern fulfillment centers utilize automated vehicles known as ____ to move goods rapidly.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"AGVs\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D cites 'automated guided vehicles (AGVs) that process up to fifty thousand parcels an hour.'\",\"evidence\":\"and automated guided vehicles (AGVs) that process up to fifty thousand parcels an hour.\"}]}]}]");
